<!DOCTYPE html><html lang="en"><head><meta charset="utf-8"><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width,initial-scale=1"><meta name="description" content="Space Explorer Issue 03: a Grade 5 illustrated science reader about the International Space Station, with four home languages and read-aloud."><meta name="theme-color" content="#101d39"><title>The International Space Station · Space Explorer Issue 03</title><link rel="stylesheet" href="styles.css?v=03.4"><link rel="stylesheet" href="../reader-navigation.css"></head><body><aside class="space-hub-bar" aria-label="Space Explorer topics"><a class="space-hub-home" href="../">← All space topics</a><div class="space-hub-links"><a href="../mars/">Mars</a><a href="../moon/">Earth’s Moon</a><a href="../iss/" aria-current="page">International Space Station</a><a href="../sun/">The Sun</a><a href="../constellations/">Constellations</a></div></aside><a class="skip-link" href="#reader">Skip to the reader</a><header><a class="brand" href="#p1">SPACE EXPLORER <span>ISSUE 03 / GRADE 5</span></a><nav aria-label="Reader sections"><a href="#p1">01 Home above Earth</a><a href="#p2">02 Why we float</a><a href="#p3">03 Space science</a><a href="#p4">04 Teamwork</a></nav></header><noscript><p class="no-script">Read the English article and <a href="iss-reader.pdf">open the illustrated PDF</a>. Enable JavaScript for translations and read-aloud.</p></noscript><details class="reader-controls" open><summary>Language &amp; listening tools</summary><div class="tools" role="region" aria-label="Language and reading controls"><div class="control-field"><label for="lang">Home language</label><select id="lang"><option value="en">English</option><option value="zh-CN">中文（简体）</option><option value="ko-KR">한국어</option><option value="ja-JP">日本語</option><option value="de-DE">Deutsch</option></select></div><div class="control-field"><label for="section">Read section</label><select id="section"><option value="0">1 · A Home Above Earth</option><option value="1">2 · Why Do Astronauts Float?</option><option value="2">3 · A Science Laboratory in Space</option><option value="3">4 · One Station, Many Countries</option></select></div><div class="control-field"><label for="voice">Reading voice</label><select id="voice"><option value="">Device default</option></select></div><div class="playback"><button id="read" type="button">▶ Read section</button><button id="pause" type="button" disabled>Ⅱ Pause</button><button id="stop" type="button" disabled>■ Stop</button></div><a class="pdf-link" href="iss-reader.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">↓ Open four-page PDF</a><span class="current-section" id="current-section">Section 1: A Home Above Earth</span><span id="status" aria-live="polite">Choose a section to hear it.</span></div><aside class="overview" id="overview" hidden aria-label="Home-language section summary"><strong>Section summary · Home-language bridge</strong><p id="summary-text"></p><small>English stays visible. Use Translate below each paragraph for help. Read section plays this summary when a home language is selected.</small></aside></details><main id="reader"><section class="page" id="p1" aria-labelledby="title-0"><div class="hero"><img src="assets/images/station.jpg" width="1041" height="694" alt="The International Space Station with its long solar arrays above the blue, curved edge of Earth." fetchpriority="high"><div class="hero-title"><div class="eyebrow">YOUR NEXT MISSION / LOW EARTH ORBIT</div><h1 id="title-0"><span>The International</span>SPACE<br>STATION</h1><p>Living, Floating, and Discovering Above Earth</p><span class="issue-tag">SCIENCE READER / 03</span></div></div><p class="cover-credit">The ISS above Earth, photographed from a departing Soyuz spacecraft by Expedition 56 crew members on October 4, 2018. Credit: NASA/Roscosmos. <a href="https://www.nasa.gov/image-article/international-space-station-as-of-oct-4-2018-5/">NASA photograph record</a></p><div class="content"><div class="eyebrow">01 / A Home Above Earth</div><h2>What is the ISS?</h2><p class="readpara" data-p="0-0">Imagine living 400 kilometers above Earth. You wake up and look out a window. Below you are blue oceans, swirling white clouds, and the curved edge of our planet. But something is strange. Your feet are not touching the floor. You are floating!</p><button type="button" class="transbutton" data-translate="0-0" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="translation-0-0">Translate this paragraph</button><p class="translation" id="translation-0-0" hidden></p><p class="readpara" data-p="0-1">Welcome to the International Space Station, or ISS. The ISS is a giant laboratory that travels around Earth. It is also a home for astronauts who live and work in space.</p><button type="button" class="transbutton" data-translate="0-1" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="translation-0-1">Translate this paragraph</button><p class="translation" id="translation-0-1" hidden></p><p class="readpara" data-p="0-2">The station moves at about 28,000 kilometers per hour. That is much faster than an airplane! It completes one orbit around Earth in about 90 minutes. Astronauts can watch about 16 sunrises and sunsets in 24 hours.</p><button type="button" class="transbutton" data-translate="0-2" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="translation-0-2">Translate this paragraph</button><p class="translation" id="translation-0-2" hidden></p><div class="facts" aria-label="Space station fact file"><div class="fact"><b>400 <span>km</span></b><small>Approximate height above Earth</small></div><div class="fact"><b>90 <span>min</span></b><small>About one orbit around Earth</small></div><div class="fact"><b>16</b><small>About 16 sunrises and sunsets in 24 hours</small></div></div><div class="callout"><b>THINK ABOUT IT</b><p>How is the ISS different from your home on Earth?</p></div></div><div class="footer"><span>SPACE EXPLORER · The International Space Station</span><b>01 / 04</b></div></section><section class="page" id="p2" aria-labelledby="title-1"><div class="content"><div class="eyebrow">02 / Why Do Astronauts Float?</div><h2 id="title-1">Why Do Astronauts Float?</h2><p class="dek">Life without an ordinary up or down</p><figure class="wide-photo"><img src="assets/images/floating.jpg" width="1041" height="694" alt="Astronaut Jessica Meir floats inside the Kibo laboratory with her hair raised and a round water sphere in front of her face." loading="lazy"><figcaption>Jessica Meir observes a floating sphere of water inside the ISS Kibo laboratory, February 9, 2020. Credit: NASA. <a href="https://www.nasa.gov/image-article/flight-engineer-jessica-meir-observes-floating-sphere-of-water/">NASA photograph record</a></figcaption></figure><p class="readpara" data-p="1-0">Imagine trying to drink a glass of water. Instead of falling into your mouth, the water might float away in little round drops! On the ISS, astronauts live in a special condition called microgravity.</p><button type="button" class="transbutton" data-translate="1-0" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="translation-1-0">Translate this paragraph</button><p class="translation" id="translation-1-0" hidden></p><p class="readpara" data-p="1-1">But does that mean there is no gravity in space? Not at all! Earth’s gravity still pulls on the station. The ISS and everything inside it are falling around Earth together. The station travels sideways so quickly that it keeps missing the ground as Earth curves away beneath it. This is why astronauts appear to float.</p><button type="button" class="transbutton" data-translate="1-1" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="translation-1-1">Translate this paragraph</button><p class="translation" id="translation-1-1" hidden></p><p class="readpara" data-p="1-2">Everyday activities become different. Astronauts use sleeping bags attached to a wall or another surface. They exercise to keep their muscles and bones strong. They must also keep food and tools from floating away.</p><button type="button" class="transbutton" data-translate="1-2" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="translation-1-2">Translate this paragraph</button><p class="translation" id="translation-1-2" hidden></p><div class="diagram-panel"><div class="eyebrow">SCIENCE SPOTLIGHT</div><h3>How orbit works</h3><img class="orbit-diagram" src="assets/orbit.svg?v=03.4" width="900" height="420" alt="Orbit teaching diagram: the ISS at the top of a circular orbit moves forward to the right. An arrow points down toward Earth’s center to show gravity. The station and everything inside fall around Earth together."><p class="caption">Teaching diagram, not to scale. Arrows show direction, not the size of a force or speed. The curved path is the orbit. Original illustration based on <a href="https://www.nasa.gov/centers-and-facilities/glenn/what-is-microgravity/">NASA’s explanation of microgravity</a>. Earth coastlines: <a href="https://www.naturalearthdata.com/about/terms-of-use/">Natural Earth</a>. <a href="assets/orbit.svg?v=03.4" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open a larger diagram</a>.</p></div><div class="callout"><b>BIG QUESTION</b><p>If gravity still exists at the ISS, why do astronauts seem weightless?</p></div><div class="mini-fact"><b>2+ hours</b><span>Astronauts exercise for at least two hours a day to help protect muscle and bone strength.</span></div></div><div class="footer"><span>SPACE EXPLORER · The International Space Station</span><b>02 / 04</b></div></section><section class="page" id="p3" aria-labelledby="title-2"><div class="content"><div class="eyebrow">03 / A Science Laboratory in Space</div><h2 id="title-2">A Science Laboratory in Space</h2><p class="dek">What can scientists discover when everything floats?</p><figure class="wide-photo"><img src="assets/images/veggie.jpg" width="1041" height="694" alt="Astronaut Soichi Noguchi holds a tray of leafy pak choi plants grown aboard the International Space Station for the Veggie investigation." loading="lazy"><figcaption>JAXA astronaut Soichi Noguchi displays Extra Dwarf Pak Choi plants grown for the Veggie study aboard the ISS, March 26, 2021. Credit: NASA. <a href="https://www.nasa.gov/image-article/jaxa-astronaut-soichi-noguchi-works-veggie-experiment/">NASA photograph record</a></figcaption></figure><p class="readpara" data-p="2-0">The ISS is much more than a place to live. It is one of the most unusual science laboratories ever built. Astronauts carry out experiments to answer questions about space, Earth, and living things.</p><button type="button" class="transbutton" data-translate="2-0" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="translation-2-0">Translate this paragraph</button><p class="translation" id="translation-2-0" hidden></p><p class="readpara" data-p="2-1">For example, scientists want to know how plants grow in microgravity. On Earth, roots usually grow downward. In space, plants cannot depend on gravity to guide their growth in the same way. Scientists also study what happens to the human body during long space missions. Astronauts exercise to help protect their muscles and bones.</p><button type="button" class="transbutton" data-translate="2-1" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="translation-2-1">Translate this paragraph</button><p class="translation" id="translation-2-1" hidden></p><p class="readpara" data-p="2-2">Other experiments investigate water, materials, and tiny living things. Some experiments can help scientists develop useful technologies or improve our understanding of health on Earth. The ISS lets scientists study questions in conditions that are difficult to maintain for long periods on Earth.</p><button type="button" class="transbutton" data-translate="2-2" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="translation-2-2">Translate this paragraph</button><p class="translation" id="translation-2-2" hidden></p><div class="callout"><b>THINK LIKE A SCIENTIST</b><p>Imagine you could send one experiment to the ISS. What would you investigate?</p><p class="frame">I would investigate ____ because I want to find out ____.</p></div><p class="closing-line">Scientists go to space to discover things that may help people everywhere.</p></div><div class="footer"><span>SPACE EXPLORER · The International Space Station</span><b>03 / 04</b></div></section><section class="page" id="p4" aria-labelledby="title-3"><div class="content"><div class="eyebrow">04 / One Station, Many Countries</div><h2 id="title-3">One Station, Many Countries</h2><p class="dek">Working together above the world</p><figure class="cupola-photo"><img src="assets/images/cupola.jpg" width="1041" height="1562" alt="Jessica Watkins floats in the Cupola, looking through its large windows at the bright blue and white Earth below." loading="lazy"><figcaption>NASA astronaut Jessica Watkins looks toward Earth through the ISS Cupola windows, May 2022. Credit: NASA. <a href="https://www.nasa.gov/image-article/astronaut-jessica-watkins-enjoys-view-of-earth-below/">NASA photograph record</a></figcaption></figure><p class="readpara" data-p="3-0">Imagine looking down at Earth from the ISS. You can see oceans, clouds, mountains, and cities. But you cannot see the borders that people draw between countries. The International Space Station is a remarkable example of countries working together.</p><button type="button" class="transbutton" data-translate="3-0" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="translation-3-0">Translate this paragraph</button><p class="translation" id="translation-3-0" hidden></p><p class="readpara" data-p="3-1">Space agencies from the United States, Russia, Japan, Canada, and Europe help operate the station. Astronauts bring different languages, cultures, experiences, and scientific knowledge.</p><button type="button" class="transbutton" data-translate="3-1" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="translation-3-1">Translate this paragraph</button><p class="translation" id="translation-3-1" hidden></p><p class="readpara" data-p="3-2">Living together in space takes teamwork. Crew members share jobs, solve problems, repair equipment, and help each other stay safe. They also communicate with scientists and engineers on Earth. The ISS has been continuously occupied since November 2000.</p><button type="button" class="transbutton" data-translate="3-2" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="translation-3-2">Translate this paragraph</button><p class="translation" id="translation-3-2" hidden></p><div class="callout"><b>MISSION QUESTION</b><p>What can working together on the ISS teach us about solving problems on Earth?</p></div><h3>Five mission words</h3><p class="support-note">Look, say, explain: use each illustration to help a partner understand the word.</p><div class="word-grid"><div class="word"><img src="assets/station.svg" width="110" height="75" alt=""><b>space station</b><span>A spacecraft where people live and work in orbit</span></div><div class="word"><img src="assets/word-orbit.svg" width="110" height="75" alt=""><b>orbit</b><span>The curved path an object follows around another object</span></div><div class="word"><img src="assets/microgravity.svg" width="110" height="75" alt=""><b>microgravity</b><span>A condition in which people and objects seem nearly weightless</span></div><div class="word"><img src="assets/astronaut.svg" width="110" height="75" alt=""><b>astronaut</b><span>A person trained to travel and work in space</span></div><div class="word"><img src="assets/experiment.svg" width="110" height="75" alt=""><b>experiment</b><span>A planned test or investigation that helps answer a question</span></div></div><h3>Think like a scientist</h3><div class="questions"><p class="question"><b>01</b> What is the ISS, and why does it travel around Earth?</p><p class="question"><b>02</b> Why do astronauts float even though Earth’s gravity still pulls on them?</p><p class="question"><b>03</b> Describe two ways life on the ISS is different from life on Earth.</p><p class="question"><b>04</b> Why is the ISS a useful place for scientific experiments?</p><p class="question"><b>05</b> How does international cooperation help astronauts live and work in space?</p></div><h3>Speak &amp; write</h3><div class="frames"><p class="frame">The ISS is ____. It travels ____.</p><p class="frame">Astronauts float because ____.</p><p class="frame">Living on the ISS is different because ____.</p><p class="frame">Scientists investigate ____ to understand ____.</p><p class="frame">Working together is important because ____.</p></div><div class="sources"><b>Explore the evidence</b><p><a href="https://www.nasa.gov/international-space-station/space-station-facts-and-figures/">NASA: station facts</a> · <a href="https://www.nasa.gov/centers-and-facilities/glenn/what-is-microgravity/">NASA: microgravity</a> · <a href="https://www.nasa.gov/missions/station/iss-research/why-do-research-on-the-international-space-station/">NASA: why research in space?</a> · <a href="credits.html">Photograph and science credits</a></p></div></div><div class="footer"><span>SPACE EXPLORER · The International Space Station</span><b>04 / 04</b></div></section></main><footer class="site-footer"><p>Issue 03 · Prepared for instructional review · English article with home-language bridges</p><p><a href="credits.html">Sources &amp; image credits</a> · <a href="iss-reader.pdf">Print edition</a></p></footer><script src="app.js?v=03.4" defer></script></body></html>